bal OEMs; supply chain for imported components in preventive health hardware","src":"concall","title":"Risks & watch-items"},{"body":"highly confident, mission-driven, emphasizing national healthcare impact; clear focus on execution, scalability, and technology leadership; avoids forward revenue guidance but projects strong order book and sector tailwinds","src":"concall","title":"Management quality / tone"},{"body":"Fischer is not just an MRI maker — it's building a vertically integrated health ecosystem: hardware (MRI/CT) + software (AI diagnostics) + services (preventive screening) + platform (FlynnCare), creating sticky, recurring revenue beyond capex sales","src":"concall","title":"Non-obvious takeaways"}],"business":[{"body":"Diagnostic Imaging: 70% of H1FY26 revenue; MRI sales (1.5T helium/helium-free, open MRI) dominate; CT and iMRI in development; Preventive Health: 30% of revenue; FlynnCare platforms for TB, oncology, mental health; subscription-based screening in schools and clinics","src":"concall","title":"Segment / subsidiary detail"},{"body":"| Challenge | Beneficiary/Loser | Dir | Conviction | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Global OEMs (GE, Siemens, Philips) dominate high-end MRI with high capex/opex | GE Healthcare | loser | High | Fischer's 20-35% lower capex and 10-15% lower opex directly erodes their pricing power in Tier-2/3 India |\n| Domestic MedTech players lack regulatory depth and AI integration | SRL Diagnostics | beneficiary | Medium | Fischer's turnkey diagnostic-preventive platforms may become preferred vendor for chain diagnostics seeking integrated solutions |\n| Government health infrastructure lacks affordable, locally made imaging | National Health Mission | beneficiary | High | Fischer is a key beneficiary of Ayushman Bharat and PLI schemes pushing domestic MRI procurement |","src":"concall","title":"Challenges → beneficiaries & losers"}],"latest_quarter":[{"body":"gross margin 28% (Q2), EBITDA 21% — driven by higher export mix, lower opex via operating leverage, indigenous manufacturing (30-35% lower capex vs GE/Siemens), and recurring revenue from preventive care subscriptions","src":"concall","title":"Margins & cost dynamics"}],"outlook":[{"body":"revenue growth: aligned with 15-20% sector CAGR; margin: sustain 20%+ EBITDA; order inflow: expected to grow with government tenders; capex/commissioning: brownfield expansion to 150-160 MRI units/year via second shift; no new greenfield announced","src":"concall","title":"Guidance"},{"body":"strong backlog to fulfill H2FY26; sales cycle 6-12 months for MRI; exact figures not disclosed due to customer sensitivity","src":"concall","title":"Order book & pipeline"},{"body":"iMRI (interoperable MRI for surgery) launch expected Q3FY26; Nanomedic skin tech (CDSCO approval pending Nov 2025); CT system commercialization; export expansion to MENA, SEA, Africa; school-based preventive health kiosks scaling in H2FY26","src":"concall","title":"Growth verticals / optionality"}]},"sources":["Q2FY26 concall summary","structured quarterly financials"],"thesis":"Fischer Medical Ventures delivered explosive 118% YoY revenue growth and 25x EBITDA expansion in Q2FY26 by scaling its indigenous MRI manufacturing and preventive health platforms, with margins now at 21% EBITDA and 16% PAT — the key shift is the dual-engine growth from diagnostic hardware and recurring preventive care models, making it a rare Indian MedTech company with both scale and profitability.","track":{"expired_n":0,"open":[],"open_total":0,"quarters_n":0,"revisions":[],"verdicts":[],"verdicts_total":0},"val5":[{"de":0.0,"fy":"FY24","mcap":2463,"opm":11.3,"pe":1368.3,"roe":4.6},{"de":0.2,"fy":"FY25","mcap":2463,"opm":4.4,"pe":2052.5,"roe":0.6},{"de":0.28,"fy":"FY26","mcap":2305,"opm":14.0,"pe":74.4,"roe":8.6}],"wtt":null},"drhp":{"rpt":{"financials":{"_source":"ledger","balance_sheet_key":[{"basis":"Standalone","cwip_amount_rs":0,"equity_share_capital_amount_rs":1700000,"fy":"FY2019","inventory